A1  - Tohyama, Harukazu
Y1  - 2022//
Y2  - 20211125//
N2  - OBJECTIVES: To investigate the knee flexor torque-angle curve after hamstring strain injury using different muscle action types and angular velocities., DESIGN: Cross-sectional., SETTING: Controlled laboratory., PARTICIPANTS: Thirteen collegiate athletes injured hamstring strain (21.0 +/- 0.8 years; 173.9 +/- 6.5 cm; 70.1 +/- 10.5 kg)., MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Concentric and eccentric knee flexor torque was measured at 60 & 300degree/sec. Peak torque and average torque every 10degree were determined from torque-angle curve and injured side was compared with non-injured side., RESULTS: No significant differences were found in the concentric muscle actions. However, the eccentric peak torque was significantly lower on the injured side at 60degree/sec (p = 0.048) and at 300degree/sec (p = 0.002). The average eccentric torque was significantly lower on the injured side at 60degree/sec from 10degree to 20degree of knee flexion (p = 0.012-0.018) and at 300degree/sec from 10degree to 60degree of knee flexion (p = 0.005-0.049)., CONCLUSION: The knee flexor torque-angle curve changes with eccentric muscle action after hamstring injury. Eccentric torque declines were close to full knee extension at 60degree/sec and a wide range of knee flexion at 300degree/sec. The assessment and rehabilitation of eccentric hamstring strength may be important to consider the effect of the angular velocity after hamstring strain injury. Copyright © 2021 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
