T1  - Influence of Altered Knee Angle and Muscular Contraction Type on Electromyographic Activity of Hamstring Muscles during 45degree Hip Extension Exercise.
A1  - Keerasomboon, Taspol
A1  - Mineta, Shinshiro
A1  - Hirose, Norikazu
Y1  - 2020//
Y2  - 20201119//
N2  - In this study, we investigated differences in electromyographic activity in the biceps femoris long head (BFl), semitendinosus, and semimembranosus muscles during 45degree hip extension with different knee angles during eccentric, concentric, and isometric hip 0degree, and isometric hip 45degree conditions with non-external resistance and 5-kg load. Twenty-two male volunteers performed 45degree hip extension with knee flexion angles of 0degree, 45degree and 90degree with non-external resistance and 5-kg load eccentric, concentric, isometric hip 0degree, and isometric hip 45degree conditions. The electromyographic data obtained during each condition were normalized with the values collected during maximal voluntary isometric contraction of each muscle. A multivariate analysis of variance with repeated measures using syntax was used to compare the normalized electromyography of each muscle across different knee joint angles in each weight condition. Electromyographic activities of the BFl, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus at 45degree and 90degree knee flexion angles were significantly greater than at 0degree in the eccentric, concentric, isometric hip 0degree, and in isometric hip 45degree conditions with both non-external resistance and 5-kg load (p<0.05), except for that of the BFl and semitendinosus in isometric hip 45degree with 5-kg load. The electromyographic activity of the BFl was significantly higher than that of the semimembranosus at 90degree knee flexion in all conditions (p < 0.05), except during eccentric with non-external resistance. There was no significant difference in electromyographic activity in the hamstring muscles among different knee angles and muscular contraction conditions. This study showed that 45degree hip extension with 45degree and 90degree knee flexion might be better in terms of the recruiting hamstring activity compared to 0degree knee flexion, regardless of the training intensity. We recommend 45degree hip extension exercises with knee flexion angles of 45degree and 90degree to activate the BFl, in preventing hamstring strain. Copyright © Journal of Sports Science and Medicine.
