T1  - ACCURACY OF THE FUNCTIONAL MOVEMENT SCREEN (FMSTM) ACTIVE STRAIGHT LEG RAISE TEST TO EVALUATE HAMSTRING FLEXIBILITY IN SOCCER PLAYERS.
A1  - Medeiros, Diulian Muniz
A1  - Miranda, Leticia Leal Prates
A1  - Marques, Vanessa Bernardes
A1  - de Araujo Ribeiro-Alvares, Joao Breno
A1  - Baroni, Bruno Manfredini
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - BACKGROUND: Poor flexibility is considered a risk factor for the hamstring strain injury, and the active straight leg raise (ASLR) test proposed as a part of the Functional Movement Screen TM (FMS TM) has been used to assess athletes hamstring flexibility. However, the accuracy of this screening test remains undescribed., PURPOSE: To examine the accuracy of the FMS TM ASLR test for assessment of hamstring flexibility in soccer players., STUDY DESIGN: Cross-sectional study., METHODS: One-hundred and one male soccer players (age, 21 +/- 3 years; height, 179 +/- 7 cm; weight, 75 +/- 9 kg) were bilaterally evaluated. All players performed a gold standard test for hamstring flexibility evaluation: the passive straight leg raise (PSLR) test measured using a gravitational inclinometer. All players also performed the ASLR test and were scored using the criteria proposed by the FMS TM., RESULTS: Of the 202 lower limbs evaluated, 17.82% scored a 1 on the ASLR [mean passive flexibility: 80.44 +/- 14.69 degree (55 degree-110 degree)], 50.99% scored a 2 on the ASLR [mean passive flexibility = 84.60 +/- 10.59 degree (56 degree-115 degree)], and 31.18% scored a 3 on the ASLR [mean passive flexibility = 92.32 +/- 11.53 degree (70 degree-120 degree)]. Limbs with FMS TM score of 3 presented significantly higher values for passive flexibility than limbs with scores of 1 and 2 (p < 0.05), but there was no significant difference between limbs with scores of 1 and 2 (p > 0.05)., CONCLUSION: The score obtained in the FMS TM ASLR test does not satisfactorily stratify the level of hamstring flexibility in soccer players., LEVEL OF EVIDENCE: 3a. Copyright © 2019 by the Sports Physical Therapy Section.
